---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About QuadletGen | Browser-Local Podman Quadlet Workbench"
	description="Learn how QuadletGen aligns with the Podman 4.4+ and 5.x systemd generator specification, resolves multi-container DNS via shared .network units, and keeps secrets in local RAM."
	canonicalPath="/about/"
	showComparison={false}
>
	<article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14 lg:px-8">
		<header class="border-b border-slate-800 pb-8">
			<p class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-cyan-400">
				Engineering &amp; Specification Documentation
			</p>
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl">
				About QuadletGen
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300 sm:text-base">
				QuadletGen (<code class="text-cyan-300">quadletgen.com</code>) is an independent, clean-room workbench that converts Docker Compose stacks and <code class="text-cyan-300">docker run</code> or <code class="text-cyan-300">podman run</code> commands into declarative Podman Quadlet systemd unit files inside browser memory.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300 sm:text-base">
			<section class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-6 sm:p-8">
				<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white sm:text-xl">
					Alignment with Podman 4.4+ and 5.x systemd generator specifications
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3">
					Starting in Podman 4.4 and maturing across Podman 5.x, upstream Podman integrated Quadlet (<code class="break-all text-cyan-300">podman-system-generator</code>) directly into its core distribution and deprecated imperative <code class="text-cyan-300">podman generate systemd</code> wrapper scripts. During every <code class="text-cyan-300">systemctl daemon-reload</code> or <code class="text-cyan-300">systemctl --user daemon-reload</code> cycle, the generator reads declarative <code class="text-cyan-300">.container</code>, <code class="text-cyan-300">.network</code>, and <code class="text-cyan-300">.volume</code> source files and compiles transient systemd service definitions matched to the installed Podman version.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3">
					QuadletGen targets documented <code class="text-cyan-300">podman-systemd.unit(5)</code> keys rather than legacy shell scripts. Service dependencies from <code class="text-cyan-300">depends_on</code> compile into <code class="text-cyan-300">[Unit] After=</code> and <code class="text-cyan-300">Requires=</code> directives. Container restart rules map to <code class="text-cyan-300">[Service] Restart=</code> supervision. Registry updates emit <code class="text-cyan-300">AutoUpdate=registry</code> alongside <code class="text-cyan-300">[Install] WantedBy=default.target</code> so rootless workloads under <code class="break-all text-cyan-300">~/.config/containers/systemd/</code> and rootful workloads under <code class="break-all text-cyan-300">/etc/containers/systemd/</code> start cleanly at boot. Operators can verify every generated file locally with <code class="break-all text-cyan-300">/usr/libexec/podman/quadlet --dryrun --user</code> prior to reloading systemd.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-6 sm:p-8">
				<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white sm:text-xl">
					Why automatic shared .network units solve inter-container DNS (podlet#190)
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3">
					One of the most frequent breakages when migrating multi-service <code class="text-cyan-300">docker-compose.yml</code> stacks to Podman Quadlet involves implicit service discovery, a recurring pain point documented in upstream community discussions including <code class="text-cyan-300">containers/podlet#190</code>. Docker Compose automatically creates a project-scoped bridge network with internal DNS even when the Compose file omits a top-level <code class="text-cyan-300">networks:</code> block. Application containers rely on that implicit bridge to reach peers by service name such as <code class="text-cyan-300">postgres:5432</code> or <code class="text-cyan-300">redis:6379</code>.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3">
					When services are translated one by one into standalone <code class="text-cyan-300">.container</code> units without a shared <code class="text-cyan-300">.network</code> unit, rootless Podman attaches each container to isolated <code class="text-cyan-300">pasta</code> or <code class="text-cyan-300">slirp4netns</code> user-mode networking where peer container hostnames cannot resolve. QuadletGen eliminates that failure mode by synthesizing a dedicated <code class="text-cyan-300">&lt;stack&gt;.network</code> unit by default and attaching every participating <code class="text-cyan-300">.container</code> unit with <code class="text-cyan-300">Network=&lt;stack&gt;.network</code> and <code class="text-cyan-300">NetworkAlias=</code> entries. Podman then provisions <code class="text-cyan-300">netavark</code> and <code class="text-cyan-300">aardvark-dns</code> on that bridge so existing database and cache connection strings keep working unchanged.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-6 sm:p-8">
				<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white sm:text-xl">
					100% browser-local execution so production secrets never leave RAM
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3">
					Real-world Compose files routinely contain database passwords, JWT signing keys, S3 credentials, internal registry hostnames, and private filesystem paths. Pasting production manifests into server-backed web converters exposes sensitive operational metadata to remote logs.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3">
					QuadletGen runs its YAML parser, CLI flag tokenizer, SELinux <code class="text-cyan-300">:z</code> and <code class="text-cyan-300">:Z</code> volume relabeler, <code class="text-cyan-300">EnvironmentFile=</code> secret splitter, and PKZIP binary packager strictly inside client-side JavaScript memory. Uploaded <code class="text-cyan-300">.yml</code> files are read via the browser File API and exported via local <code class="text-cyan-300">Blob</code> URLs without transmitting container definitions over the network. Read our <a href="/privacy/" class="font-medium text-cyan-300 underline decoration-cyan-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-cyan-200">Privacy Policy</a> for full details on how you can verify zero-upload behavior in your browser DevTools Network tab.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-6 sm:p-8">
				<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white sm:text-xl">
					Independent open-source workbench and non-affiliation notice
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3">
					All parser logic, unit formatters, built-in templates, and documentation on QuadletGen are original MIT-licensed software built for Linux administrators running Fedora Server, Fedora CoreOS, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, CentOS Stream, Debian, Ubuntu, openSUSE MicroOS, and Universal Blue. There are zero container count limits, zero account walls, and zero paid tiers. To keep hosting sustainable, deployment documentation may feature clearly labeled infrastructure referral links governed by our <a href="/terms/" class="font-medium text-cyan-300 underline decoration-cyan-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-cyan-200">Terms of Service</a>.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/80 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-400 sm:text-sm">
					<strong class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Non-affiliation notice.</strong> QuadletGen (<code class="text-slate-300">quadletgen.com</code>) is an independent engineering project and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Red Hat, Inc., IBM, the Podman project, the <code class="text-slate-300">containers/podlet</code> maintainers, Docker, Inc., or the Linux Foundation. Podman and Red Hat are trademarks or registered trademarks of Red Hat, Inc. in the United States and other countries. Docker is a trademark or registered trademark of Docker, Inc.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-6 sm:p-8">
				<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white sm:text-xl">
					Contact and specification feedback
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3">
					Found an edge case in a Compose directive, need a new Podman 5.x unit key mapped, or have a security question? Email the maintainer directly at <a href="mailto:hello@quadletgen.com" class="font-mono font-medium text-cyan-300 underline decoration-cyan-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-cyan-200">hello@quadletgen.com</a> with a sanitized snippet and your target Linux distribution plus <code class="text-cyan-300">podman --version</code> output.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
					<a
						href="/"
						class="rounded-lg bg-cyan-400 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-950 transition-colors hover:bg-cyan-300"
					>
						Open Compose to Quadlet Workbench
					</a>
					<a
						href="/privacy/"
						class="rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-200 transition-colors hover:border-cyan-500/50 hover:text-white"
					>
						Privacy Policy
					</a>
					<a
						href="/terms/"
						class="rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-200 transition-colors hover:border-cyan-500/50 hover:text-white"
					>
						Terms &amp; Affiliate Disclosure
					</a>
				</div>
			</section>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
